      "statement": "The actual Gibbs mu=volume.tilted(-U) is probability and id belongs to L2(mu). For every direction v, covarianceBilin(mu)(v,v)>=norm(v)^2/beta. This is the linear-observable covariance corollary of Cramer-Rao, not the full nonlinear-observable theorem or a matrix-inverse result.",
      "formula": "\\mu(dx)=Z_U^{-1}e^{-U(x)}dx,\\quad X\\in L^2(\\mu),\\quad \\forall v,\\ \\operatorname{Cov}_{\\mu}(\\langle v,X\\rangle,\\langle v,X\\rangle)\\ge\\beta^{-1}\\|v\\|^2.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Derive actual Gibbs probability and score second moment",
          "text": "The existing canonical Gibbs gradient theorem derives the positive integrable exponential weight and actual score-square integrability from C2 Hessian bounds, using genuine weighted domination and full-space score integration by parts. Reuse that producer, without supplying a probability or moment certificate.",
          "formula": "0<Z_U<\\infty,\\quad \\mu\\text{ probability},\\quad\\mathbb E_\\mu\\|\\nabla U\\|^2\\le\\beta d.",
          "lean": "gibbs_gradient_moment; weighted_gradient; directional_ibp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Derive position L2 without a minimizer",
          "text": "Strong gradient monotonicity between zero and x gives alpha norm(x)<=norm(gradient U(x))+norm(gradient U(0)). Square this and dominate by the integrable score square plus a constant under the actual probability law. Thus id is genuinely L2 and its Bochner mean exists. Zero norm is handled separately.",
          "formula": "\\|x\\|^2\\le\\frac{2}{\\alpha^2}(\\|\\nabla U(x)\\|^2+\\|\\nabla U(0)\\|^2),\\quad X\\in L^2(\\mu).",
          "lean": "gibbs_position_l2; StrongConvexFirstOrder.gradient_inner_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn; memLp_two_iff_integrable_sq_norm"
        },
        {
          "title": "Prove centered linear-score pairing with all IBP products integrable",
          "text": "For a unit direction v put p=E_mu X, q(x)=inner(x-p,v), a(x)=DU(x)[v]. Actual position and score L2 imply q, a, qa are integrable as required. The full-space integration-by-parts API is applied only after deriving the weighted q, weight times derivative q, and derivative weight times q integrability. Since Dq[v]=1 and D(exp(-U))[v]=-exp(-U)a, normalize by the true positive Z_U to obtain E_mu qa=1. No mean-score-zero hypothesis is needed.",
          "formula": "\\mathbb E_\\mu[q a]=1,\\quad q=\\langle X-\\mathbb EX,v\\rangle,\\quad a=DU(X)[v],\\quad\\|v\\|=1.",
          "lean": "coordinate_position_ibp; integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable; integrable_tilted_iff; integral_tilted"
        },
        {
          "title": "Bound the actual directional score second moment",
          "text": "The existing genuine score IBP identifies the weighted score square with the actual Hessian directional integral. The upper Hessian beta bound and probability normalization give E_mu a^2<=beta. All score-square, Hessian and product integrability are proved before integral algebra.",
          "formula": "\\mathbb E_\\mu a^2=\\mathbb E_\\mu D^2U(X)[v,v]\\le\\beta.",
          "lean": "directional_ibp; integral_mono; integral_tilted; gibbs_covariance_unit"
        },
        {
          "title": "Integrate an actual nonnegative square",
          "text": "Expand the integrable nonnegative square (a-beta q)^2. Combining E qa=1 with E a^2<=beta yields beta^2 E q^2>=beta. Positivity of beta follows from 0<alpha<=beta, so divide to get variance>=1/beta. Actual covarianceBilin is identified with the centered q square using proved id L2.",
          "formula": "0\\le\\mathbb E(a-\\beta q)^2=\\mathbb Ea^2-2\\beta+\\beta^2\\mathbb Eq^2\\le-\\beta+\\beta^2\\mathbb Eq^2\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\operatorname{Cov}_\\mu(v,v)\\ge\\beta^{-1}.",
          "lean": "gibbs_covariance_unit; integral_add; integral_sub; covarianceBilin_apply"
        },
        {
          "title": "Scale every direction and include dimension zero",
          "text": "Handle v=0 directly. For v nonzero use u=v/norm(v), apply the unit-direction result and use the true covariance bilinear map scaling. This quantifies all v without assuming a unit vector exists, so dimension zero is included. This does not establish general nonlinear Cramer-Rao or any Brascamp-Lieb upper covariance.",
          "formula": "\\operatorname{Cov}_\\mu(v,v)=\\|v\\|^2\\operatorname{Cov}_\\mu(v/\\|v\\|,v/\\|v\\|)\\ge\\|v\\|^2/\\beta.",
          "lean": "gibbs_covariance_lower; map_smul; smul_apply"
